## Claim under pressure
SPRINT **2.9** (MED): println walker → full `c_secret_emit_guard`.
Zero-trust gates for **this** close (not 2.8 expand, not 2.10 wrappers,
not 5.8 `${}`, not T1 return lattice completeness):

| # | Gate | Expectation |
|---|------|-------------|
| Z1 | Path-A bare SECRET IDENT at `println` / `eprintln` refuse | Dual-path `ERR\tsecret` + rc≠0 (check + emit-c + emit-llvm) |
| Z2 | **Every** product println shape fail-closed | Multi-arg, concat, sink-alias, **later-arg after inner RPAREN**, expr-pos |
| Z3 | No FP on string / comment spelling of a SECRET name | `println("tok")` ALLOW; no `ERR secret` |
| Z4 | Do not reopen 2.8 | No expand-hole relitigation; MACRO residual stays 2.8 |
| Z5 | emit-c compile rail: SECRET bytes never become `oo_print_*` of a SECRET IDENT | Stmt `c_emit_println` or front-door refuse before print |

Adversarial stance: treat the check-path println walker as a **desync
weapon** until every SECRET IDENT in a `println`/`eprintln` argument
list is refused on **check and every product emit**. Treat “LLVM/wasm
only print the first arg” as **accidental drop**, not a refuse.
Treat “stmt println is a different helper” as **unify residual** only
when the other rail still **BLOCK**s. A check `OK` on
`println((n), tok)` with `// SECRET: tok` is **FN**.

